type LoginRequest struct {
	Password string `json:"password"`
	Username string `json:"username"`
}

// MarketValueGap Why a row's market valuation cell is not the figure a reader expects — see Position.market_value_gap, the one field that publishes it. The values split in two, and `market_value_minor` tells the halves apart without ambiguity. THE FIRST THREE SAY THERE IS NO VALUATION AT ALL, and on a row carrying one of them market_value_minor, market_value_currency, price, price_on and unrealized_pnl_minor are all null. `type_not_priced`: this program computes no value for the instrument's type. It prices share, etf and bond, and nothing else — currency, crypto, metal and custom have no valuation model, and neither has any type added to InstrumentType without one. A QUOTE MAY WELL EXIST FOR SUCH A ROW and changes nothing, which is the whole reason this value exists: a client captioning it «no quote» would send the reader off to wait for data that is not missing (#78). Nothing is coming here — no decision to write such a model has been taken — so this value must never be captioned as a gap that will close. `no_face_value`: the instrument is a bond and the catalog records no face value for it. A bond's quote is a PERCENTAGE of face value rather than money (see Position.price), so with no face value there is nothing to take the percentage of, and an arriving quote closes nothing. face_value_minor and face_currency are stored both-or-neither (a CHECK constraint since migration 0012), so one value answers for both halves. `no_quote`: this program does price the instrument, its catalog row holds everything the valuation needs, and no price has been stored for it yet. THIS IS THE ONLY ONE OF THE THREE A QUOTE CLOSES, and that is exactly what it means. THE FOURTH SAYS THERE IS A VALUATION AND IT COULD NOT TRAVEL. `no_rate_valuation_currency`: the fx table could not convert the valuation out of the currency it is denominated in, so it stayed in market_value_currency instead of reaching the position's own `currency` (which is where market_value_minor is normally brought, so that the row can compare it with cost_minor), and on any row that publishes a PositionInBase at all, its market_value_minor is null with its unrealized_pnl_minor behind it. Here market_value_minor is NOT null — the raw, unconverted figure is published rather than hidden. EXACTLY ONE VALUE IS PUBLISHED, and the order above is the order the server decides in — the same rule InBaseGap follows, for the same reason: a cause an arriving quote would not close is reported ahead of one it would, so a crypto row with no quote says `type_not_priced` and a face-value-less bond with no quote says `no_face_value`, neither of them promising that a quote is what is missing. `no_quote` is therefore a positive statement and not a leftover: reaching it means the type is priced and the catalog row is complete.
type MarketValueGap string

